implementationPlan.note:
CONSUMER EDGE LANDED, and admission-wait gained NO authority.

DIRECTION OF THE EDGE - the design decision this step turns on. The wiring runs supersession -> admission-wait, not the reverse: `carryWaitProjection` in packages/scheduler/src/supersession/supersession-dispositions.ts imports and CALLS admission-wait's own `validateIntentionalWait`, then binds the validated wait's `ownerNodeKey` to the produced disposition set. That is one durable production call site (a real import in a production module, not a test) and it satisfies the step's hard constraint by construction: admission-wait.ts gained no import, no function, no clock, no enqueue and no transition. Its header invariants ("Nothing in this module schedules, enqueues, or transitions anything", "There is no clock here") are untouched and still true. Had the edge been wired the other way it would have needed admission-wait to reach into supersession authority, which the step says to report blocked over rather than do.

admission-wait.ts CHANGE IS THE SCOPE COMMENT ONLY - a header edit, zero executable lines. The stale deferral line "supersession carry of wait/blocker projections -> graph supersession engine" is REMOVED from the "EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE, owned elsewhere" list and replaced with a "CONSUMED BY" paragraph naming the landed owner by file and function and stating that the direction is deliberate. A reader now cannot conclude the edge is still missing. Verify with `git diff -- packages/scheduler/src/admission/admission-wait.ts`: comment lines only.

SCOPE DISCLOSURE: admission-wait.ts is NOT in this task's declared owned paths (supersession/**, index.ts, index-surface.test.ts), but DoD 5 and this step direct the edit explicitly and name the file and line. Flagging it rather than silently widening scope. The edit is comment-only and reddens nothing - the admission suite is green.

THE EDGE IS PROVEN REAL, four cases, and one of them can only pass if the REAL validator ran:
1. a wait whose owner node has a disposition carries, and the returned disposition's nodeKey is asserted to be the wait's ownerNodeKey;
2. a wait whose recheckAtGate is BEYOND its deadlineGate refuses. This is admission-wait's own ADMISSION_WAIT_HORIZON_INVALID branch and is invisible to any shape check. Per the "refusal answered by an earlier guard" hazard I did NOT stop at "it refused": the test now calls `validateIntentionalWait(unbounded)` directly and asserts the issue codes are exactly ["ADMISSION_WAIT_HORIZON_INVALID"], and asserts the unmodified fixture validates ok - so the case is pinned to the horizon rule and cannot be passing on some unrelated malformation;
3. an ORPHAN wait - well formed, owner node absent from the set - refuses PLANNING_DISPOSITION_UNKNOWN at SCHEDULER_SUPERSESSION_SET, so a projection with no backing disposition never carries into the successor;
4. a producer refusal propagates through the edge with code AND layer intact: an UNKNOWN attempt fact reaches the caller as {PLANNING_DISPOSITION_UNKNOWN, SCHEDULER_SUPERSESSION_ATTEMPT}, i.e. the family layer, not flattened into the set layer.

